EvoBuddy @VERSION@ (macOS @ARCH@)
=================================

A UI study build: the interface of a desktop client, reproduced natively in
GTK 3. Wording and icons are placeholders; no brand assets are reproduced.

Self-contained
--------------
EvoBuddy.app carries GTK 3, every library it needs, an icon theme, compiled
GSettings schemas and its input-method modules. It wants macOS @MACOS@ or
newer and nothing else — no Homebrew, no pkg-config, no GTK, no fontconfig
and no fonts need to be installed. Fonts are macOS's own, selected through
CoreText, which is what renders text on every Mac anyway.

It was verified by auditing every load command in the bundle and by running
exactly this .app with dyld's own load log watched, which is the only way a
"self-contained" claim means anything.

Run
---
    open EvoBuddy.app            # or double-click it; drag to Applications first if you like

The first launch, macOS will warn that the app "cannot be opened because the
developer cannot be verified" — it is signed ad-hoc, not notarized. Either
right-click the .app and choose Open once, or:

    xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine EvoBuddy.app

From a terminal (the way it is tested):

    ./EvoBuddy.app/Contents/MacOS/evobuddy

Known gaps
----------
Text is drawn with the macOS system font stack, so line breaking and label
widths differ slightly from the Linux bundle, which carries its own fonts for
deterministic metrics. The Linux bundle's `run` launcher does the same job as
the Contents/MacOS/evobuddy script here; neither writes anything outside the
user's cache directory.
